## Explanation
A pointer's size is determined by the architecture of the processor and the memory model of the operating system, not the data type it points to. On a 64-bit system, a pointer typically occupies 8 bytes, while on a 32-bit system, it occupies 4 bytes, regardless of whether it is an `int*`, `char*`, or `void*`. This uniformity is necessary because the pointer must be large enough to hold any valid memory address within the system's address space. Note that using `sizeof` on a pointer will return this architecture-dependent size, whereas `sizeof` on the dereferenced type returns the size of the underlying data.

## Example
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int *ptr_int;
    double *ptr_dbl;

    // Both pointers occupy the same amount of memory 
    // regardless of the size of the types they point to.
    printf("Size of int*: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(ptr_int));
    printf("Size of double*: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(ptr_dbl));

    // Contrast this with the size of the data types themselves
    printf("Size of int: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(int));
    printf("Size of double: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(double));

    return 0;
}
```

