## Explanation
To safely manage heap-allocated memory, you must ensure that every `malloc` or `calloc` call is paired with exactly one `free` call to prevent memory leaks. After deallocation, the pointer becomes a "dangling pointer," as it still holds the address of memory that the allocator may have already reclaimed or re-assigned. To prevent use-after-free vulnerabilities and double-free errors, you must explicitly set the pointer to `NULL` immediately after calling `free`. Since `free(NULL)` is a defined no-op in C, this practice provides a robust safeguard against accidental attempts to access or free the same memory region twice.

## Example
```c
#include <stdlib.h>

void process_data() {
    int *ptr = malloc(sizeof(int) * 10);

    if (ptr == NULL) return; // Always check allocation success

    // ... perform operations ...

    free(ptr);
    ptr = NULL; // Prevent dangling pointer usage

    // Subsequent calls to free(ptr) are now safe no-ops
    free(ptr); 
}
```

