## Explanation
A `const` pointer in C is defined by the placement of the `const` qualifier relative to the pointer operator (`*`). A `const int *ptr` (pointer to constant) prevents modification of the underlying data, while an `int * const ptr` (constant pointer) prevents modification of the pointer's address itself. These qualifiers are enforced by the compiler at compile-time to ensure memory safety and data integrity, though they do not provide true runtime immutability, as the underlying memory can still be modified via non-const aliases or pointer casting. Understanding the distinction is critical for API design, particularly when enforcing read-only access to buffers or preventing accidental pointer reassignment in embedded systems.

## Example
```c
int val1 = 10;
int val2 = 20;

// Pointer to constant: cannot modify *ptr1, but can reassign ptr1
const int *ptr1 = &val1; 
ptr1 = &val2; // Valid

// Constant pointer: cannot reassign ptr2, but can modify *ptr2
int * const ptr2 = &val1;
*ptr2 = 30; // Valid
// ptr2 = &val2; // Compiler Error: assignment of read-only variable

// Double const: neither the address nor the value can change
const int * const ptr3 = &val1;
```

