Remember that a dangling pointer occurs when a pointer continues to hold a memory address after the variable it was pointing to has been destroyed or removed from scope. Since local variables are destroyed when a function ends, any pointer that was holding the address of that local variable becomes "dangling"—it points to a location that no longer belongs to your program. Accessing this memory is dangerous because it can lead to unpredictable behavior or crashes.

```c
int *ptr;
{
    int x = 10;
    ptr = &x; 
} // x is destroyed here; ptr is now dangling
```

